----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3: can the core eta be measured to +-0.1, as the item asks?  Three error terms, counted separately.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (a) the QUOTED statistical errors on M_HSE, M_gas and M_star, propagated by Monte Carlo;
  (b) the MASS-RECONSTRUCTION systematic: X-COP publishes five mass profiles per cluster (forward, NFW, Einasto,
      isothermal, Burkert).  They agree at R500 by construction and diverge in the core.  Counted TWO ways, because
      lumping them all together would overstate it: the CUSPED set (forward/NFW/Einasto, all of which fit these
      clusters well) is the fair systematic; the cored isothermal and Burkert profiles are shown separately, since
      they are poor fits and their disagreement is a statement about them, not about the core mass;
  (c) the stellar import for the five clusters without a stellar profile (excluded from the headline above).
  canonical: median statistical sigma(eta) in the core = 0.231;  half-range over the three CUSPED reconstructions = 0.668;  over all five = 1.471
  alt      : median statistical sigma(eta) in the core = 0.217;  half-range over the three CUSPED reconstructions = 0.626;  over all five = 1.382
  at 50 kpc the three cusped X-COP mass reconstructions span a factor 1.52 (range 1.09-2.39); including the two cored ones, 5.07 (2.01-8.70).  At R500 they agree by construction.
  [PASS] 67 the item's own bar CANNOT be met AGAINST INTEREST -- 'core eta measured to +-0.1 in each cluster' is out of reach by an order of magnitude, and not because of photon noise.  The statistical error is already several times the bar, and the systematic from which hydrostatic mass reconstruction one adopts is larger still: even restricted to the three well-fitting cusped profiles they disagree by tens of percent at 50 kpc, which is what a hydrostatic mass in a cool core is worth   (statistical sigma(eta) = 0.23; cusped-model half-range 0.67; all-five half-range 1.47 (canonical).  Model spread at 50 kpc: x1.52 cusped, x5.1 including cored)

  (c) how much did the stellar import matter?  Recomputing the SEVEN measured clusters with item 18's global
      M_star/M_gas = 0.047 instead of their own stellar profiles, to size the bias the core would have carried:
  canonical: core eta with the global stellar import = 4.13 against 2.91 with the measured stellar profiles -- a +42% bias, in the direction of OVERSTATING the framework's problem
  alt      : core eta with the global stellar import = 3.86 against 2.72 with the measured stellar profiles -- a +42% bias, in the direction of OVERSTATING the framework's problem

