  the AeST-condensate phase-pinning lever claims 2.3e+13-3.4e+13 Msun inside 420 kpc, quoted as 23-34% of an ASSUMED 1e+14 Msun residual for a 1e+15 Msun host (CLUSTER_PHASE_PINNING_POLYTROPE.md) -- a number STANDING.md has already WITHDRAWN as live, used here only to size the target.
  the lever's captured mass is set to the cosmic dust share inside R500, so it scales with the host: for these clusters' median M500 = 5.66e+14 it would be 1.30e+13-1.92e+13 Msun.
  the residual it would have to fill, MEASURED here rather than assumed, is 8.72e+13 Msun -- 1.54x the mass-scaled version of the paper's assumed 1e14, because the deficit does not fall as fast as the host mass.
  [PASS] 67 the core lever, sized against a measurement rather than an assumption, covers LESS than the 23-33% it was quoted at: on these twelve clusters it would supply 15-22% of the deficit actually measured inside its own 420 kpc core radius.  AGAINST INTEREST, and consistent with STANDING.md having already withdrawn the number; the honest reading is that the framework's cluster core is at least 80% open, not 67% open   (measured deficit inside 420 kpc = 8.72e+13 Msun (range 4.76e+13-1.72e+14) at median M500 = 5.66e+14; mass-scaled lever 1.30e+13-1.92e+13 = 15-22% coverage)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 5: the LambdaCDM side on the same rows.  Does a cold-dark-matter core have the same problem?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  X-COP fits an NFW to each cluster's own hydrostatic mass profile, so the question is whether that fit RECOVERS
  the model-independent core mass.  If it does, LambdaCDM has no core problem in these clusters and the deficit
  above is the framework's alone; if it does not, the core is hard for both.
  NFW-fit / forward-method mass at 30-100 kpc: median 1.00, 16-84% [0.74, 1.27] over 48 cluster-radius pairs
  the NFW fit's own dark fraction inside 30-100 kpc: median 0.85 (i.e. LambdaCDM puts 85% of the core mass in dark matter, which is what a cusp does)
  [PASS] 67 the alternative computed beside AGAINST INTEREST -- LambdaCDM is not in trouble here and the comparison is not even close: an NFW fitted to the same clusters recovers the model-independent core mass to a factor 1.00 on the median, because a cusp supplies core mass for free.  The framework must find the same mass in baryons with a kernel boost that has largely switched off at these radii, and it does not   (median M_NFW/M_forward = 1.00 at 30-100 kpc; NFW dark fraction there = 0.85; framework eta there = 2.91)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 6: is the CORE residual a function of the framework's own variable?  (Item 18 asked this outside 0.2 R500
and answered NO -- r/R500 organised eta better than g_bar/a_0.  Extended inward here.)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  over 30-420 kpc in the seven clusters with measured stars (63 points): scatter of log eta at fixed g_bar/a_0 = 0.078 dex; at fixed r/R500 = 0.073 dex
  d log eta / d log(g_bar/a_0) over the core = +0.113; y spans 0.19 to 1.44, so the core is NOT deep-MOND -- nu runs 1.43 to 2.82 there and the kernel supplies little
  [PASS] 67 shape -- the core extends item 18's finding rather than overturning it: inside 420 kpc the residual is organised no better by the framework's acceleration variable than by the cluster's own radius, which is what an extra MASS component looks like and not what a missing acceleration looks like   (scatter of log eta: 0.078 dex at fixed g_bar/a_0 vs 0.073 dex at fixed r/R500 (r/R500 wins))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mutation controls
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M67b-1 mutation -- nu = 1 must give back the plain Newtonian core discrepancy, and does; the gap between the two numbers is the entire work the kernel does at 30-100 kpc, and it is small   (nu = 1 gives 6.11, the Route A kernel gives 2.91 (the kernel removes 52% of the core discrepancy; at R500 item 18 found it removes ~67%))
  [PASS] M67b-2 mutation -- pairing each cluster's hydrostatic mass with ANOTHER cluster's baryons must widen the eta distribution, and it does, so the numbers above are properties of the individual clusters and not of the estimator   (shuffled scatter of log eta = 0.150 +- 0.026 dex vs the real 0.085 dex; 100% of 200 shuffles are worse)
  [PASS] M67b-3 mutation -- a wrong a_0 (ten times canonical) must move the core eta, and it does, so the kernel is live at these radii and the deficit above is not the trivial statement that nu = 1 in a cluster core   (core eta = 2.91 at the canonical a_0, 2.72 at the alt footing, 1.13 at 10 a_0)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  67 = a LIABILITY, measured rather than extrapolated, on a substitute sample.
  * The item as posed (HFF lensing cores from `hff_granata_*.tsv`) remains NOT RUNNABLE -- those tables are member
    photometry with no mass column; h67_hff_cores.py documents that with the numbers and it is not revisited here.
  * On X-COP, which has both a hydrostatic mass profile from 30 kpc and a gas (and for seven, a stellar) mass
    profile, the framework's core residual is eta = 2.91 (canonical) / 2.72 (alt) at 30-100 kpc,
    i.e. AT LEAST AS LARGE as the eta ~ 2 the same clusters give at R500 -- the residual does not close inward.
  * The kernel is nearly inert there: y = g_bar/a_0 is of order 1-3 in the core, so nu removes only
    52% of a Newtonian discrepancy of 6.1.
  * The item's own precision bar (+-0.1 per cluster) is UNREACHABLE with hydrostatic masses: even the three cusped
    X-COP reconstructions differ by x1.52 at 50 kpc (x5.1 including the cored fits), a
    systematic sigma(eta) = 0.67 against a statistical 0.23.  A core eta to +-0.1 needs LENSING,
    which is what the item wanted the HFF clusters for and
    what the on-disk HFF tables do not contain.  That is the concrete thing to fetch next: a published cumulative
    projected M(<R) or convergence map for A2744 / AS1063 / M0416 / M1149, plus an X-ray gas profile into the core.
  * Against interest twice over: the core deficit is not one number (factor 2.2 across clusters), and LambdaCDM's cusp
    covers the same core mass without strain.

